# Line endings are decided here rather than by each machine's Git configuration.
#
# Without this file, `core.autocrlf=true` gave every Windows working tree CRLF while CI checked the same files
# out as LF on Linux. Two consequences, one cosmetic and one not:
#
#   * every commit printed "LF will be replaced by CRLF" for every file it touched, which is noise that trains
#     you to ignore warnings;
#   * anything line-ending sensitive behaved differently locally than in CI, and scripted multi-line edits
#     (`perl -0pi -e 's/...\n.../'`) silently matched nothing on Windows and everything on Linux. A
#     substitution that quietly changes no bytes is worse than one that fails, because the check that follows
#     it passes for the wrong reason.
#
# So text is LF everywhere, including in the working tree, and the exceptions below are the files where the
# platform genuinely cares.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows shells are the exception. Batch interpreters are sensitive to line endings around labels and `goto`,
# and there is no reason to find out the hard way — these are launched by double-click on Windows and nowhere
# else.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary, so Git must never look inside or translate anything.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.docx binary
*.zip binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
*.db binary
